 ## tab-keys.mjs
 
-### §stable-tab-keys — closing a tab never renumbers the others
-- Since: 2026-09-02
-- Decision: a key is assigned once from `next` and is never reused or shifted. Closing a tab leaves a gap (`t1,t3`).
-- Forbidden: renumbering the remaining keys after a close to make them contiguous.
-- Reason: the key is the React key and the URL state. Shifting keys remounts every tab to the right of the closed one
-  and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29, reproduced by the "close keeps keys" test).
-- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs`
+### §contiguous-tab-keys — closing a tab renumbers the others (replaces §stable-tab-keys)
+- Since: 2026-10-22 (the maintainer overrode §stable-tab-keys, in force since 2026-09-02)
+- Decision: `closeTab` renumbers the remaining tabs in order so the keys stay contiguous (`t1,t2,…`), and resets
+  `next` to `tabs.length + 1` so the next open continues the sequence.
+- Rejected: the earlier rule that a key is assigned once and never reused or shifted, with gaps (`t1,t3`) left
+  after a close.
+- Reason: maintainer decision to keep keys contiguous.
+- Known cost: the key is the React key and the URL state, so a close changes the key of every tab to the right of
+  the closed one. Those tabs remount and lose scroll position and unsaved form input. This is the behavior behind
+  the 2026-08-29 incident that led to §stable-tab-keys, and this change brings it back.
+- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs` ("close renumbers keys", "open after close stays contiguous")
